\f3\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f0\fs22 The Cloudborne came away glad in their hearts that The Dawkins would deliver them from the religion of Latter Day Capitalism (
\f1\i Praise him!
\f0\i0 ); the Elvi heard that The Dawkins would deliver science and reason to vanquish The Holy Rollers (
\f1\i Praise him!
\f0\i0 ); and the Airborne kids, who had never been so toxically drunk or so ecstatically down in the mud in their lives, just wanted to stick it up their parents\'92 tax-deductible bribery accounts.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was a warm, balmy night in November, in which activism has been para-scientifically proven to foment. A few few thousand of the crowd loitered while the crew packed away gear and started to pull down the stage. Elvi went amongst the crowd with guitars\'97many who had not performed on stage\'97and entertained small audiences unwilling to let the night end. Out of respect for the High Elvis, they all adhered to the first little law and sang the words of the Great Teacher, thereby rendering the other two surplus to the occasion. Or rather, the 
\f1\i mostly 
\f0\i0 sang the Great Teacher\'92s lyrics, having decided The Sages little ditty was unacceptable too, as it had been allowed by the High Elvis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Around four in the morning\'97which in Elvi time is regarded as still last night\'97security began chasing the stragglers off The Domain. The young Airborne, unused to being chased off anything, began rounding everyone up with a plan to relocate the party. And so hundreds of concert goers herded themselves to hover-pad stalks where Airborne kids used the credentials in elevators to ferry every Elvis and Cloudborne up to the centre of the business and political district, above the Layer, into a clear night of stars, moonlight, gleaming towers, street lights that actually worked, and something that hit the back of their throats like ice, but just turned out to be fresh air.\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::2>
\f0\fs22 Music and singing continued through the night where they settled on the eastern end of Bent Street Plaza at the the base of TRAMP Tower. From the beginning, the Airborne exercised the allowance accounts, and ordered the night to be comfortable. It started with blankets and pillows delivered by bewildered and sometimes threatened events planners. Someone ordered catering-grade umbrella/patio heaters. Later, couches arrived and got passed overhead like crowd-surfing hippopotamuses until someone decided they\'92d rather sit in them right there, thank you very much. As dawn broke, somebody ordered a marquee. Someone else, gloriously, hired a yurt. The Elvi called The Ghetto for coffee urns and barbecues, each  large enough to cook a water buffalo and three small crocodiles. Airborne kids offered to buy the provisions and boggled when they read the Elvi\'92s grocery list.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::2><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f0\fs22 \'93This will be better than drugs,\'94 one of them said. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An Elvis grabbed the list back and scrawled 
\f1\i amphetamines 
\f0\i0 across the bottom.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::2>
\f0\fs22 The Airborne kids were not disappointed, determined to eat everything offered them like tourists at a food orgy. The bacon was properly burned black, the eggs hard-scrambled and the pork sausage patties shallow fired on the plates in the tide of residual bacon fat. Then the peanut butter and banana sandwiches were flattened on the hotplates and singed crisp. For anyone brave enough there was fool\'92s gold loaf. Though the Cloudborne were wary, the Airborne ate everything, that is until they were offered buttermilk biscuits in sawmill gravy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::2><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f0\fs22 Everyone drank coffee. They were going to need it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They Elvi called for more instruments and the means to amplify them. They ordered tatami mats and taught karate while they set up an improvised stage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then someone realised they\'92d better get portaloos.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::2>
\f0\fs22 On a Sunday, the central business and political district above the Layer was a ghost town, but it\'92s doubtful anyone would have sent the riot squad in when your shareholders\'92 children formed nearly half the protest. No one wanted to see their quarterly reports catch fire, since the kids had discovered what money genuinely bought, which was that nobody was allowed to discipline them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::2><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f0\fs22 After breakfast, a lad painted 
\fs20 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 OCCUPY BENT STREET
\fs22  across a tarpaulin in dripping black, and stood back from it, pleased. A girl beside him read it twice and shook her head, and painted her own slogan on another tarp: 
\fs20 GET BENT
\fs22 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Messages went out on tablets, with photographs and an address. The preferred headline by a ration of more than fifteen to one was 
\fs20 GET BENT.
\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 <!$Scr_Ps::1>
\f0\fs22 Sunday found the plaza  \'97 organised, the way a pot of soup organises itself if you leave it on low, . And Sunday came up over the top of the Layer the way it never came up underneath it: raw, total and free, a whole morning of the good light spilling along a plaza built to keep it for the few. handing out the good sunscreen \
Along the kerb the rubbish stood in neat bags, because the High Elvis had asked half a million people to take their rubbish with them when they left the Domain and they had, to the last wrapper \'97 against the frontage of the Directorates like sandbags against a flood. Clean the Domain, he had told them, like he had told the ELvi to clean the Ghetto\
The other Elvi currency was story, and it got spent around the barbecue as the second day wore on. An Elvine with the trailer at her back and a queue in front of her told the tale of the Dawkins to a rapt semicircle of Airborne kids \'97 the one long blind accident, the survival of the fittest, the Holy Momma who bore the first of them in a shotgun house with the power off \'97 and the kids listened, chins on knees, to a doctrine of natural selection recited to several hundred people who had never once been selected against, and found it, they said, incredibly deep. Nobody was converted. It was not that kind of night. The stories went round the way stories go round a fire, because a fire is where a people keeps the things it cannot afford to lose, and these three peoples were, for one weekend only, keeping theirs in the same place.\
By Sunday afternoon there were T-shirts. A Cloudborne kid had a cousin with a press and a van, an Airborne kid had an account with no discernible floor, and between the anger of the one and the capital of the other a run of shirts came up the goods lift reading GET BENT in a foot of black type and got handed out across the plaza for nothing at all. Free. It confused people.  the boy who had paid for them thought charging would have been embarrassing\
.\
